#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f5f9e59-3c36-5807-b8f1-60d5c867aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef51c8-be4a-5a5f-b4b6-7b12be0bb5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399583} {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} {:answer/id #uuid "49678131-628e-553a-9844-5179f76e8b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399585} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d4bea4-992f-5fb1-be9b-5bc63898ddea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7c9128-4613-5828-914f-598e6ef01189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d44d9a6b-035e-5168-a3f6-72ab2e21a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106878" :question/problem "The rate at which a body cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the body in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241219} {:answer/id #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241380} {:answer/id #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242096} {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} :answer-field/id #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12647afe-fb4f-5f2b-9326-19bf07c40a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397886} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd2a414-b8e6-52ba-9a39-7d6bbb93cce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397887} {:answer/id #uuid "6612145e-ab64-58c5-8ac9-574cb5ec22a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397888} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e45ac-2ae6-5e93-b31c-67cedb5a0fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59f961-3401-5e50-8b21-92d3facaf654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342581f4-cabc-5059-a251-2692ca6f27ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321542} {:answer/id #uuid "71be98e8-1e44-50bb-9bb9-b32dd2edc859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322040} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e6a8e3-742a-59bc-850f-65fe5a3e9e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322796} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e99ed-fb8f-592b-af00-7b9afa2790e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322949} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b01d91-18e1-599b-8abf-64d7291ac4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"}]}
